# Jackson @JacksonInject Deserialization Override Vulnerability

## Overview

In Jackson Databind (particularly versions < 2.12), `@JacksonInject` annotated constructor parameters can be overridden by JSON input. This allows attackers to bypass server-side configuration by providing values directly in the JSON payload.

## How @JacksonInject Works

`@JacksonInject` marks a constructor parameter or field to be populated from an `InjectableValues` provider (e.g., Guice) rather than from JSON input.

```java
@JsonCreator
public MyClass(
    @JsonProperty("name") String name,
    @JacksonInject MyConfig config  // Should come from DI, not JSON
)
```

## The Vulnerability

### Root Cause

In Jackson 2.10.x, `@JacksonInject` has a `useInput` attribute (`OptBoolean`):
- `OptBoolean.DEFAULT` (default) — falls back to Jackson's default behavior
- `OptBoolean.TRUE` — always use JSON input
- `OptBoolean.FALSE` — never use JSON input, only injection

The problem: `AnnotationIntrospector.findInjectableValue()` base implementation calls `findInjectableValueId()` and creates a `JacksonInject.Value` with `_useInput = null`:

```java
// Base AnnotationIntrospector
public JacksonInject.Value findInjectableValue(AnnotatedMember m) {
    Object id = findInjectableValueId(m);
    if (id != null) {
        return JacksonInject.Value.forId(id);  // _useInput = null!
    }
    return null;
}
```

When `_useInput` is null, the parameter is treated as BOTH injectable AND accepting JSON input.

### Attack Vector

An attacker provides JSON with an empty key `""` that maps to the unnamed `@JacksonInject` parameter:

```json
{
  "dimension": "x",
  "function": "malicious_code",
  "": {"enabled": true}
}
```

The empty key `""` matches the `config` parameter (which has no `@JsonProperty` name), overriding the injected `JavaScriptConfig(enabled=false)` with `JavaScriptConfig(enabled=true)`.

## Fix Approach

### Override `findInjectableValue()` in the Annotation Introspector

Instead of only overriding `findInjectableValueId()`, override `findInjectableValue()` to explicitly set `useInput = false`:

```java
@Override
public JacksonInject.Value findInjectableValue(AnnotatedMember m) {
    if (m.getAnnotation(JacksonInject.class) == null) {
        return null;
    }
    // ... compute Guice Key ...
    return JacksonInject.Value.construct(injectId, Boolean.FALSE);
}
```

This ensures that `@JacksonInject` parameters NEVER accept JSON input, only values from the DI container.

### Key Classes

| Class | Role |
|---|---|
| `JacksonInject` | Annotation with `useInput` attribute |
| `JacksonInject.Value` | Value class with `_useInput` field and `willUseInput()` method |
| `AnnotationIntrospector` | Base class, `findInjectableValue()` and `findInjectableValueId()` |
| `NopAnnotationIntrospector` | No-op base for custom introspectors |
| `InjectableValues` | Provider of injectable values |

## References

- CVE-2021-25646 (Apache Druid)
- Jackson Databind issue: https://github.com/FasterXML/jackson-databind/issues/962

# Druid's Guice-Jackson Integration

## Overview

Apache Druid integrates Google Guice (dependency injection) with Jackson (JSON serialization) through custom annotation introspection and injectable values. This integration allows Jackson to use Guice-managed beans during JSON deserialization.

## Key Components

### GuiceAnnotationIntrospector

Located at `core/src/main/java/org/apache/druid/guice/GuiceAnnotationIntrospector.java`

Extends `NopAnnotationIntrospector` and overrides `findInjectableValueId()` to map `@JacksonInject` annotations to Guice `Key` instances.

```java
public class GuiceAnnotationIntrospector extends NopAnnotationIntrospector {
    @Override
    public Object findInjectableValueId(AnnotatedMember m) {
        if (m.getAnnotation(JacksonInject.class) == null) return null;
        // Look for @BindingAnnotation for qualified injection
        // Return Key.get(type) or Key.get(type, annotation)
    }
}
```

### GuiceInjectableValues

Located at `core/src/main/java/org/apache/druid/guice/GuiceInjectableValues.java`

Implements Jackson's `InjectableValues` interface, resolving injectable values from the Guice `Injector`:

```java
public class GuiceInjectableValues extends InjectableValues {
    @Override
    public Object findInjectableValue(Object valueId, ...) {
        if (valueId instanceof Key) {
            return injector.getInstance((Key) valueId);
        }
        throw new IAE("Unknown class type...");
    }
}
```

### DruidSecondaryModule

Located at `core/src/main/java/org/apache/druid/guice/DruidSecondaryModule.java`

Registers the introspector and injectable values with the ObjectMapper:

```java
private void setupJackson(Injector injector, final ObjectMapper mapper) {
    final GuiceAnnotationIntrospector guiceIntrospector = new GuiceAnnotationIntrospector();
    mapper.setInjectableValues(new GuiceInjectableValues(injector));
    mapper.setAnnotationIntrospectors(
        new AnnotationIntrospectorPair(guiceIntrospector, mapper.getSerializationConfig().getAnnotationIntrospector()),
        new AnnotationIntrospectorPair(guiceIntrospector, mapper.getDeserializationConfig().getAnnotationIntrospector())
    );
}
```

## Deserialization Flow

1. Jackson encounters a `@JacksonInject` annotated parameter
2. `GuiceAnnotationIntrospector.findInjectableValueId()` returns a Guice `Key`
3. Base `AnnotationIntrospector.findInjectableValue()` wraps this in `JacksonInject.Value.forId(key)` — with `_useInput = null`
4. Jackson registers the parameter as both injectable AND accepting JSON input
5. During deserialization, if JSON provides a value for the parameter, it overrides the injection
6. If no JSON value, `GuiceInjectableValues.findInjectableValue()` resolves via Guice

## Security Implication

The gap between steps 3 and 4 is the vulnerability. By overriding `findInjectableValue()` to return `JacksonInject.Value.construct(id, Boolean.FALSE)`, we tell Jackson to NEVER use JSON input for `@JacksonInject` parameters.

## Fix Pattern

```java
// In GuiceAnnotationIntrospector
@Override
public JacksonInject.Value findInjectableValue(AnnotatedMember m) {
    if (m.getAnnotation(JacksonInject.class) == null) {
        return null;
    }
    // ... compute Guice Key (same logic as findInjectableValueId) ...
    return JacksonInject.Value.construct(guiceKey, Boolean.FALSE);
}
```

This centralizes the fix — all classes using `@JacksonInject` automatically benefit without any per-class changes.

